<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jugend (magazine)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1896–1940</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jugend was an illustrated weekly magazine of art and literature founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1896 by the publisher Georg Hirth. Known for its decorative covers and satirical tone, it gave its name to Jugendstil, the German form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>. It was published until 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugend-magazine.jpg" alt="Jugend (magazine)" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Dannenberg (1871–?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fritz_Dannenberg_-_Jugend._M%C3%BCnchner_illustrierte_Wochenschrift,_1897.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
